lesson:
## Delegates are typed function values

```csharp
Func<int, int> square = x => x * x;        // takes an int, returns an int
Action<string> log = msg => Console.WriteLine(msg);  // returns nothing
Predicate<Order> isPaid = o => o.Status == "paid";   // same as Func<Order, bool>
square(4)   // 16
```

LINQ, `List.RemoveAll`, `Task.Run`, minimal API handlers and middleware are all delegates. `Func<..., TResult>` returns a value; `Action<...>` does not.

## Closures and the capture trap

A lambda captures **variables**, not values. In a `for` loop there is ONE loop variable:

```csharp
var fs = new List<Func<int>>();
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) fs.Add(() => i);
fs.Select(f => f())          // 3, 3, 3

foreach (var x in new[] { 0, 1, 2 }) fs2.Add(() => x);   // 0, 1, 2: foreach gets a fresh variable per iteration (since C# 5)
```

Fix the `for` case by copying into a local inside the loop: `var copy = i; fs.Add(() => copy);`. Captured variables also keep objects alive as long as the delegate lives.

## Multicast delegates

`+=` combines delegates; invoking runs them all, in order:

```csharp
Action<string> a = s => log.Add("A" + s);
a += s => log.Add("B" + s);
a("x");                       // A then B
Func<int> f = () => 1; f += () => 2;
f()                           // 2: only the LAST return value comes back
```

## Events

```csharp
public class Publisher
{
    public event EventHandler<int>? Changed;
    public void Raise(int n) => Changed?.Invoke(this, n);   // ?. : safe with no subscribers
}
pub.Changed += OnChanged;
pub.Changed -= OnChanged;
```

An `event` is a delegate field that outsiders can only `+=` and `-=`: they cannot invoke it or overwrite the list. Handlers run **synchronously** on the raising thread, in subscription order; an exception in one stops the rest.

## Unsubscribe, or leak

The publisher's delegate list holds a reference to every subscriber. A long-lived publisher (a static cache, a singleton service) keeps short-lived subscribers (pages, view models, per-request objects) alive forever unless they unsubscribe.

`-=` only removes a handler that is **the same delegate**: `p.Changed -= (_, x) => n += x;` creates a NEW lambda and removes nothing. Keep a reference to the handler, or return a subscription object:

```csharp
public IDisposable Subscribe(Action<decimal> handler)
{
    EventHandler<decimal> h = (_, p) => handler(p);
    PriceChanged += h;
    return new Subscription(() => PriceChanged -= h);
}
using var sub = feed.Subscribe(p => Console.WriteLine(p));   // disposed: unsubscribed
```

This is the pattern behind `IObservable`, `IOptionsMonitor.OnChange` and many client libraries.

